{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Therefore.</b> The reason [for lakhen]: though you do not request a sign, He will give it to you. The wonder is those who say he speaks of their god — yet the sign was given to Ahaz, and he [the child] was born after many years. Moreover, he said: for before the boy knows good and evil, the land shall be abandoned — yet the land of Ephraim and also Damascus was abandoned in the sixth year of Hezekiah, and Scripture says 'before the two kings.' Many erred and said that Immanuel is Hezekiah, but this is impossible: if we say this prophecy was at the beginning of Ahaz's reign, his entire reign was sixteen years, and when he died Hezekiah was twenty-five years old. Others said he is Ahaz's son; others said he is a parable for the kingdom — if so, what is the meaning of 'the boy knowing good and evil,' and what is the meaning of 'Maher-shalal-hash-baz' (Isaiah 8:4)? The straight interpretation in my eyes is that Immanuel is Isaiah's son; likewise Maher-shalal, and the proof is 'and I drew near to the prophetess' (Isaiah 8:3); likewise She'ar-yashuv. Each of these three sons was called a sign for what was to come: Immanuel — that HaShem will help them and be with them in the distress they stand in because of the two kings; Maher-shalal — that the time of Samaria's exile has come; She'ar-yashuv — that the remnant of Israel will repent. The faithful witness for this interpretation: 'Here am I and the children whom HaShem has given me' (Isaiah 8:18), like 'the children whom Elohim has graciously given' (Genesis 33:5). The commentators who explain them as disciples — I will show their like in Scripture; Isaiah too was 'for a sign and a portent upon Egypt' (Isaiah 20:3). The Gaon [Saadia] said the sign is that it will be a male. In my opinion the sign is 'he shall eat curd and honey,' for it is not the way of infants to eat these at birth." "We know that the young one in years is called na'ar and the female na'arah; likewise 'almah, and the male 'elem. It is possible for her to be a virgin [betulah] or a married woman [be'ulah], for the meaning refers only to the years; and 'elem proves it — 'and the way of a man with an 'almah' (Proverbs 30:19), and she is not a virgin at all, because he said at the beginning 'I have not known them' [i.e., he did not know her?]." "<b>And you shall call.</b> The 'almah [is addressed], as in 'and your people shall sin' (Exodus 5:16)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "Reading צ\"ל נקרא (in brackets in the source) as a corrected reading: the text originally read נקראו ('they were called') but should read נקרא ('he was called'). I translate per the corrected singular.", 1 "The last clause quoting Proverbs 30:19 is textually difficult; the argument as transmitted is obscure, possibly corrupt. Translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:47.960241465Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}